Selected epoch 1; validation NLL 2.488208; inference training NLL 2.384083; test NLL 2.447731; test accuracy 0.156250.
\image(figures/deformable_conv_v2_training.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Explicit 4-epoch Adam fit and a tensor-computed true/predicted-class count matrix at the selected checkpoint. Training uses augmentation and batch BN moments; validation/test use saved moving moments. Counts sum to 64; IDs follow the original CIFAR-10 order.)
\alttext(Explicit 4-epoch Adam fit and a tensor-computed true/predicted-class count matrix at the selected checkpoint. Training uses augmentation and batch BN moments; validation/test use saved moving moments. Counts sum to 64; IDs follow the original CIFAR-10 order.)


> Observed subset result

V1: validation selects epoch 2, with validation NLL 2.362737, inference training NLL
2.297858, test NLL 2.402730 and 14.0625% test accuracy on 64 images.

V2: validation selects epoch 1, with validation NLL 2.488208, inference training NLL
2.384083, test NLL 2.447731 and 15.6250% test accuracy on 64 images.

Four epochs are only 64 updates, so both fits stay close to chance (ten classes give
NLL 2.30 and 10% accuracy); the loops show the explicit optimizer at work, not a trained model.

These fixed-seed subset results do not establish a ranking of attention mechanisms or
deformable variants. Validation selection and training/inference BN differences matter;
the small test split is not a published benchmark.

> Native confusion matrices

These matrices use the held-out predictions from the executed program above.
The native `ConfusionMatrix` SVG API labels exact counts with true classes
running from top to bottom and predicted classes from left to right.

\image(figures/deformable_conv_confusion_api_0.svg)
\caption(Held-out classification confusion counts, evaluation 1. Exact cell counts are printed in the image.)
\alttext(Integer prediction counts with true classes as rows and predicted classes as columns.)


> Conclusion

Learned displacements change sampling locations, and modulation independently scales taps.
Controlled geometry, independent bilinear values and all offset-bias finite differences make
those operations concrete. Complete compact v1/v2 fits expose the tensor optimizer and all
predictors while keeping their small-data scope separate from detection benchmarks.

> References

- [Dai et al. (2017), Deformable Convolutional Networks](https://arxiv.org/abs/1703.06211).
- [Zhu et al. (2019), Deformable ConvNets v2](https://arxiv.org/abs/1811.11168).
- [Deformable ConvNets release](https://github.com/msracver/Deformable-ConvNets).
- [CIFAR-10 dataset](https://www.cs.toronto.edu/~kriz/cifar.html).
